    "q-253552": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253552",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot n:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{1+1}\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot 3 & =3 \\\\ (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot 4 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
