    "q-253138": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253138",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ),\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14165783/assets/bcece2e5bdef3c9d939d953fbeb0c5163d9343004cd309f28db37bf9b5b089ef.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating, non-convergent sequence. Moreover, $A$ is unbounded below. In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that ${a}_{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any real number $x.$ Therefore, $A$ has no lower bounds and $inf(A)\\,=-\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-253138-0",
