    "q-253141": {
      "dom_id": "step-q253141",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14165783/assets/8152677cc952ca219aa9395f3d1a9acbc9379b1cffd0b0f0e6bd0f3f0148632a.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?$\\,{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-253141-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
